 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the octree depth and the cutoff threshold. The cutoff reduces the number of nodes visited for pixels with low $y$ values (assuming $y=0$ is top or bottom depending on coordinate system) [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the octree and the texture array. The traverser stack depth is limited by the cutoff, reducing peak memory usage compared to full traversal [G].
-*   **Strengths:** Reduces computation for parts of the image where deep traversal is not necessary (e.g., distant or less detailed regions if mapped to $y$) [G].
-*   **Limitations:** The quality of the result depends heavily on the mapping between $y$-coordinate and depth. If the scene geometry does not correlate with screen height, the cutoff may introduce artifacts or miss important details [G].
-*   **When to Use:** When the scene has a strong correlation between vertical screen position and depth/detail requirements, or when performance is critical and some loss of detail is acceptable [G].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When uniform rendering quality is required across the entire image [G].
